From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Send what the link will carry when the receiver chose a poor one

Which band a cast runs on is the receiver's decision. A group owner that
answers on one of the social channels and never moves the group off it puts
the whole cast on 2.4 GHz at twenty megahertz wide, where there is a fraction
of the room a cast was measured to need. The same receiver, the same room and
the same settings gave sixty frames a second out of sixty on 5 GHz and
thirteen out of sixty twenty minutes later on 2.4, with the band the only
thing that had changed. On the poor link the encoder was throwing away
between four and seven frames in ten to keep up, and what arrived was a
slideshow.

Nothing in the engine can see which happened. The frequency is in the kernel
and reachable by anyone who asks it over netlink, but NetworkManager
publishes no frequency at all for a Wi-Fi Direct device and wpa_supplicant's
own bus refuses anyone who is not root. The client can see it. So the client
says so, and this is told rather than asking.

Being told, a cast asks for a mode and a rate the poor link will carry:
thirty frames a second at 1280x720, and four megabits. Fewer frames rather
than fewer pixels, because what is being shared is a desktop, and text that
stays sharp while moving at thirty is worth more than text that is smeared
while moving at sixty. The larger modes stay behind it for receivers that
offer nothing else.

It travels the way the other thing a helper must know before it has done
anything travels: an argument on the method, into the environment the helper
is started with, read once while the mode is chosen and once while the rate
is set. Both happen before there is a cast to change.

